# component: tabs
# decisions: A11Y-20, API-5, API-25, API-29, DOC-1, DOC-2, FOC-1, FOC-6, FRM-4, LAY-14
# hunks:
#   API-29: `TabsList` gains a `size` axis (`default` | `sm`, written as `data-size`); `sm` is the
#     compact h-7 list for scope/view tabs in a FilterBar, and its triggers take `px-2` and 14px
#     icons. Tabs keep the one focus ring left in the system, from base.css (FOC-13).
#   A11Y-20: `Tabs` passes `orientation={orientation}` to `TabsPrimitive.Root` as well as writing
#     `data-orientation`. Upstream destructures the prop for the attribute and never forwards it,
#     so Base UI kept `horizontal`: a vertical list announced no `aria-orientation` and moved on
#     left/right. The layout classes are untouched; they still read the root's attribute.
#   LAY-14: `tabsListVariants` gains an `overflow` axis (`visible` | `scroll`), and `TabsList`
#     resolves it to `scroll` for `variant="line"` and `visible` otherwise, written as
#     `data-overflow`. `scroll` is `AttachmentGroup`'s recipe (`min-w-0 scroll-fade-x
#     scrollbar-none overflow-x-auto overscroll-x-contain`) plus `max-w-full` (a `w-fit` flex row's
#     fit-content never narrows below its min-content), `justify-start` (a centred overflowing row
#     clips its first tab), `overflow-y-hidden`, and `bottom-[-4px]` on the triggers' line
#     indicator, which upstream draws 1px below the list and a scroll box would clip — every one
#     behind `not-data-vertical:`, so a vertical list keeps upstream's layout and its inline-end
#     indicator, while a list that carries no orientation at all (the route-tabs recipe's plain
#     `div`) scrolls as the horizontal list it is, without the consumer writing the attribute.
#     The 1px lift targets every direct child (`[&>*]`), not only `data-slot=tabs-trigger`, so a
#     route list's links keep their indicator inside the scroll box too. `relative` makes the list the triggers' offset parent, which Base UI's own
#     composite measures against when it scrolls the roving stop into view on mount and on arrow
#     keys; without it that scroll lands on the wrong offset. The one thing the engine does not do —
#     keep the SELECTED tab in view when the value changes from outside — is `useActiveTabInView`,
#     a `MutationObserver` on `aria-selected` that moves only the list's own `scrollLeft`, never the
#     page. The list's `ref` is merged with the internal one through `mergeRefs` from our npm layer,
#     and `React` is imported for the hooks.
#   API-25: `TabsTrigger`'s class string moves verbatim into an exported `tabsTriggerVariants`
#     recipe, beside upstream's own exported `tabsListVariants`, so route tabs (links in a `nav`)
#     are drawn from the system's classes rather than a copy of them.
#     Because a route `nav` is not a Base UI root, the horizontal geometry both recipes read from the
#     `group/tabs` ancestor — the list's `h-8`, the line indicator's `inset-x-0`, `h-0.5` and
#     `bottom-[-5px]` — is gated on `group-not-data-vertical/tabs:` instead of
#     `group-data-horizontal/tabs:`, so a nav that carries no orientation draws as the horizontal
#     tabs it is. A vertical root still selects upstream's vertical geometry.
#   FOC-1, FOC-6: `focus-visible:border-ring focus-visible:ring-[3px] focus-visible:ring-ring/50
#     focus-visible:outline-1 focus-visible:outline-ring` goes from `TabsTrigger`, and
#     `outline-none` goes from `TabsContent` — Base UI gives the panel a tab stop, so suppressing
#     the outline would leave a focusable element with no affordance at all.
#   FRM-4: `disabled:pointer-events-none` and `aria-disabled:pointer-events-none` are dropped, so
#     a disabled tab stays hoverable and a Tooltip can explain why. Both opacity halves stay.
#   API-5: NO HUNK, and that is the audit § 5.2 asks for ('re-confirm each is still meaningful').
#     A `loading` prop belongs on a control that commits something and must hold its box while it
#     does — Button and Toggle. A tab reveals a panel that is already mounted; the pre-reset Tabs
#     never had `loading` either. The panel, not the tab, is where a pending state belongs.
#   A11Y-2: audited, no hunk. The trigger is `h-[calc(100%-1px)]` inside an `h-8` list, so its
#     target is 31px; `tabs.test.tsx` measures it.
#   cn: `import { cn } from "cn"` becomes `@vegastack/design` — our npm layer owns `cn` (DOC-2).
#   formatting: the repository formatter (prettier defaults: semicolons, trailing commas)
#               reflows the file, and `registry-stamp.mjs` writes the provenance header the
#               three-copy model needs (DOC-1); those hunks carry no decision of their own.
